1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:13,000
You only had to look up into the sky to be enthralled by their passing, mesmerized by their mythic proportions, and giddy at the promise of their future.

2
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:21,000
They were the rigid airships, one of the most conspicuous and exciting chapters in the history of aviation.

3
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Now, the great airships on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
In 1929, the Graf Zeppelin flew around the world with 20 passengers, almost four years

5
00:00:46,460 --> 00:00:49,580
before the first solo flight around the globe by an airplane.

6
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:59,060
For 12 and a half days, the Graf's passengers dined on gourmet meals, listened to music,

7
00:00:59,061 --> 00:01:06,000
and looked out the window as they passed leisurely over regions that no human being had ever seen from the air before.

8
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:15,620
This was airship travel at its peak, an evolutionary voyage that began with a basic balloon and a lot of hot air.

9
00:01:16,740 --> 00:01:26,440
With no place to go but up in 1783, the Montgolfier brothers created the first balloon that rose under its own power, hot air.

10
00:01:26,441 --> 00:01:31,520
They were constantly going around with sponges and water to put out small fires.

11
00:01:31,860 --> 00:01:34,836
In December of that year, French chemist J.A.

12
00:01:34,837 --> 00:01:41,580
Charles made the first manned flight in a balloon filled with hydrogen, a much lighter source of lift.

13
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:51,240
It was about seven times lighter than air, and it didn't take much to figure out that that would help support flight and especially manned flight.

14
00:01:51,241 --> 00:01:56,180
But hydrogen was considered flammable air because it burns furiously.

15
00:01:56,440 --> 00:01:58,900
especially when mixed with oxygen.

16
00:02:00,280 --> 00:02:03,280
The problem now facing the early aeronauts

17
00:02:03,281 --> 00:02:05,560
was how to contain the lighter-than-air,

18
00:02:05,740 --> 00:02:07,900
highly flammable hydrogen gas.

19
00:02:09,040 --> 00:02:12,440
Fabric technology had progressed by that time,

20
00:02:12,520 --> 00:02:14,460
and they were starting to rubber coat balloons.

21
00:02:14,860 --> 00:02:18,320
They found out, once you flew, let's go somewhere.

22
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:19,100
We want to fly.

23
00:02:19,580 --> 00:02:22,080
The balloon itself was a pretty limited vehicle.

24
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:23,340
After all, you couldn't steer it.

25
00:02:23,540 --> 00:02:26,020
It simply floated to wherever the wind dug it.

26
00:02:26,440 --> 00:02:33,540
A handful of aviators believed that the best hope for controlled flight was a motorized, steerable balloon, or dirigible.

27
00:02:35,460 --> 00:02:40,460
Among them was a dashing young Brazilian balloonist named Alberto Santos Dumont,

28
00:02:40,461 --> 00:02:47,420
who said trying to propel a dirigible balloon through the air is like pushing a candle through a brick wall.

29
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:54,300
In 1884, two members of the French Army's Balloon Corps achieved an aeronautical first

30
00:02:54,301 --> 00:03:01,240
when they navigated their electric-powered balloon back to its original starting point, against the wind.

31
00:03:02,260 --> 00:03:07,640
The 23-minute flight was cut short by the limited life of the engine's 500-pound batteries.

32
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:11,860
Engine weight haunted the airship pioneers.

33
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:15,680
The challenge now was to create a balloon that was not only steerable,

34
00:03:16,120 --> 00:03:20,080
but streamlined and strong enough to lift men and machines.

35
00:03:20,081 --> 00:03:25,080
By 1897, Santos Dumont began work on his first airship.

36
00:03:25,081 --> 00:03:34,080
Santos made a great leap forward in engine technology using a motorcycle-type motor for his airship

37
00:03:34,081 --> 00:03:40,080
and did some innovative work in propeller design that gave his ship greater efficiency.

38
00:03:40,081 --> 00:03:45,080
It was a smaller engine, giving him more lift, available lift for payload or for passengers.

39
00:03:45,081 --> 00:03:45,827
passengers.

40
00:03:45,828 --> 00:03:51,800
In order to maintain the internal gas pressure of the elongated and egg-shaped balloons,

41
00:03:51,801 --> 00:03:58,440
aerial pioneers added ballonets or airbags that expand and contract to compensate for changes in

42
00:03:58,441 --> 00:04:00,886
the pressure of the lifting gas.

43
00:04:00,887 --> 00:04:05,320
This basic principle is still used in dirigibles or blimps

44
00:04:05,321 --> 00:04:06,021
today.

45
00:04:06,022 --> 00:04:12,040
Soon the French added a rigid load-bearing keel to the elongated balloon to help distribute

46
00:04:12,041 --> 00:04:15,220
the enormous weight of the early electric and steam engines.

47
00:04:16,260 --> 00:04:19,680
These semi-rigid airships were quite successful

48
00:04:19,681 --> 00:04:21,960
and used as military lookouts.

49
00:04:22,940 --> 00:04:25,940
And then a man by the name of Count Zeppelin came along

50
00:04:25,941 --> 00:04:28,640
and said, what would happen if we took a whole bunch of balloons

51
00:04:28,641 --> 00:04:31,320
and put them together and put a framework around them?

52
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:34,400
And from that was born the Zeppelin airship.

53
00:04:35,580 --> 00:04:40,760
On July 2, 1900, a feisty Count Ferdinand von Zeppelin

54
00:04:40,761 --> 00:04:44,680
staked his fortune and reputation on a crazy idea.

55
00:04:45,700 --> 00:04:48,240
Three years before the Wright brothers' first flight,

56
00:04:48,380 --> 00:04:52,660
the Count's first rigid airship took flight over Lake Constance in southern Germany.

57
00:04:53,380 --> 00:04:57,820
It flew for 18 minutes and a distance of 3 1⁄2 miles.

58
00:04:58,840 --> 00:05:03,860
The LZ-1 for Luftschiff Zeppelin I was 416 feet long,

59
00:05:04,440 --> 00:05:06,040
38 1⁄2 feet in diameter,

60
00:05:06,160 --> 00:05:10,560
and had a lifting capacity of more than 27,000 pounds.

61
00:05:10,760 --> 00:05:23,115
Rather than fill one airbag with hydrogen as his predecessors had, the Count inflated 17 large gas cells and enclosed them in a rigid aerodynamic structure of aluminum

62
00:05:23,116 --> 00:05:26,000
How did he do it? alloy, or derailment.

63
00:05:26,340 --> 00:05:32,120
The airship was powered by two engines with the combined horsepower of a Volkswagen Beetle.

64
00:05:32,940 --> 00:05:40,560
At the turn of the century, it was the invention of the internal combustion engine that helped make the Count's dream of a rigid airship a reality.

65
00:05:40,760 --> 00:05:43,020
But they were expensive to build.

66
00:05:44,260 --> 00:05:50,300
The German military said if Count Zeppelin can keep his airship in the air for 24 hours endurance,

67
00:05:50,920 --> 00:05:54,040
that they would be willing to purchase then his next airship.

68
00:05:54,960 --> 00:06:00,440
During one 24-hour endurance flight, Count von Zeppelin experienced engine trouble

69
00:06:00,441 --> 00:06:04,540
and put the airship down near the Daimler Engine Works and went to launch.

70
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:06,920
It would prove fortuitous.

71
00:06:06,921 --> 00:06:10,920
In the meantime, a storm came and blew the airship away.

72
00:06:12,360 --> 00:06:19,120
Most unfortunate for the count because it didn't fulfill his 24-hour endurance requirement for government sponsorship.

73
00:06:19,900 --> 00:06:27,060
But what happened was a spontaneous flood of donations came from the German people to help pay for the next airship.

74
00:06:27,760 --> 00:06:34,540
He received about 7.5 million gold marks from the loss of his ship and from all the donations that came in

75
00:06:34,541 --> 00:06:37,980
to build the Zeppelin company and to build future airships.

76
00:06:37,981 --> 00:06:40,140
It was a technical marvel.

77
00:06:40,141 --> 00:06:43,140
As the Zeppelin began to work the bugs out

78
00:06:43,141 --> 00:06:45,780
and began to be used more or less routinely,

79
00:06:45,781 --> 00:06:47,780
just before World War I,

80
00:06:47,781 --> 00:06:50,780
there was a German Zeppelin commercial service

81
00:06:50,781 --> 00:06:52,100
called the DeLag.

82
00:06:52,101 --> 00:06:57,900
By 1914, the DeLag airships carried more than 34,000 passengers,

83
00:06:57,901 --> 00:07:00,300
over 100,000 miles.

84
00:07:00,301 --> 00:07:04,540
Without a single accident, it was an economical success.

85
00:07:04,541 --> 00:07:13,620
With the start of World War I, the Daylog commercial service came to an end,

86
00:07:14,040 --> 00:07:16,620
and Zeppelins were used for military purposes.

87
00:07:17,520 --> 00:07:24,700
The Zeppelin was touted as the great secret weapon that was going to revolutionize warfare.

88
00:07:25,420 --> 00:07:28,160
The German rigid airships reigned supreme.

89
00:07:28,860 --> 00:07:31,180
Capable of flying above 11,000 feet,

90
00:07:31,181 --> 00:07:35,200
They were well out of the range of ground-based anti-aircraft guns,

91
00:07:35,500 --> 00:07:38,440
and not a single airship was lost to enemy fire.

92
00:07:38,960 --> 00:07:43,520
The German military ordered 26 airships during the first year of the war,

93
00:07:43,640 --> 00:07:46,000
and the Zeppelin company went into high gear.

94
00:07:46,920 --> 00:07:50,220
Zeppelins were used by the German military for reconnaissance,

95
00:07:50,400 --> 00:07:53,120
as well as bombing raids over Great Britain.

96
00:07:53,700 --> 00:07:55,480
They were above the clouds oftentimes.

97
00:07:56,300 --> 00:07:59,180
They did try using radio as a means of navigation,

98
00:07:59,181 --> 00:08:03,260
and many a raid took place by a Zeppelin

99
00:08:03,261 --> 00:08:06,840
in which the commander would report that he raided this place or that place

100
00:08:06,841 --> 00:08:08,180
and he was nowhere near it.

101
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:09,540
There was no bombing accuracy.

102
00:08:10,020 --> 00:08:13,620
On the other hand, the airships did a large amount of reconnaissance work,

103
00:08:13,920 --> 00:08:17,500
basically flying over the sea, watching out for the British Navy.

104
00:08:19,340 --> 00:08:23,620
As the war escalated, the Germans built lighter and lighter airships,

105
00:08:23,621 --> 00:08:27,340
capable of operating at an altitude of 20,000 feet.

106
00:08:27,341 --> 00:08:34,340
Frustrated by their lack of rigid airship technology, the British concentrated on building fighter planes,

107
00:08:34,341 --> 00:08:41,340
capable of climbing to 22,000 feet, and ammunition that could bring the high-flying Zeppelins down.

108
00:08:41,341 --> 00:08:49,340
The thing that really did the airships in during the First World War was the development by the British of the so-called incendiary bullet.

109
00:08:49,341 --> 00:08:56,340
A bullet like this hitting an airship filled with hydrogen meant one bullet was all it took to bring an airship down in flames.

110
00:08:57,340 --> 00:09:03,080
It was not the great Zeppelin airships that revolutionized warfare in World War I.

111
00:09:03,820 --> 00:09:07,140
Instead, it turned out to be a small, incendiary bullet.

112
00:09:10,420 --> 00:09:16,600
In March of 1917, at the age of 78, Count von Zeppelin died.

113
00:09:17,320 --> 00:09:21,620
Two Zeppelins dropped flowers as his coffin was lowered into the earth.

114
00:09:21,621 --> 00:09:30,500
The British flew eight rigid airships during World War I, while the Germans built and improved

115
00:09:30,501 --> 00:09:41,480
more than 100 of them.

116
00:09:41,481 --> 00:09:46,240
At the end of World War I, the great German Zeppelin company was forbidden from building

117
00:09:46,241 --> 00:09:49,860
any more airships by the Treaty of Versailles.

118
00:09:49,861 --> 00:09:54,360
The remaining Zeppelin airships were to be divided up among the Allies.

119
00:09:54,361 --> 00:10:00,700
But the German airship crews basically destroyed the majority of the airships in their hangars

120
00:10:00,701 --> 00:10:03,600
before the treaty went into effect.

121
00:10:03,601 --> 00:10:08,360
The airships that the Germans did give over, for example, to the French, they didn't give

122
00:10:08,361 --> 00:10:12,820
them the technology as to how to operate them and how to maintain them.

123
00:10:12,821 --> 00:10:15,860
That they kept unto themselves.

124
00:10:15,861 --> 00:10:19,900
By 1921, the United States was interested in getting its own airship.

125
00:10:20,720 --> 00:10:24,200
They ordered a British-built version of the high-flying German airships,

126
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:30,280
and during the final stress test at sea, the R-38 snapped in two, killing 44 sailors.

127
00:10:31,220 --> 00:10:34,660
The airship's light frame buckled under the pressure of high-speed turns.

128
00:10:35,960 --> 00:10:38,800
Despite considerable apprehension from the Allies,

129
00:10:39,400 --> 00:10:44,540
the United States ordered an airship from the cash-strapped Zeppelin Company as part of war reparations.

130
00:10:45,140 --> 00:10:47,460
The ship was to be delivered in 1922.

131
00:10:48,640 --> 00:10:52,660
In the meantime, the Americans decided to build their own airship.

132
00:10:53,040 --> 00:10:54,660
They would call it the Shenandoah.

133
00:10:55,220 --> 00:10:58,380
During the war, a German Zeppelin came down in France,

134
00:10:58,460 --> 00:11:00,300
and the Allies had a chance to inspect it.

135
00:11:00,860 --> 00:11:01,980
This was the L-49.

136
00:11:02,780 --> 00:11:05,420
And as a result, they generated some drawings.

137
00:11:05,421 --> 00:11:07,520
The Navy acquired these drawings

138
00:11:07,521 --> 00:11:11,140
and basically built their own Zeppelin in Philadelphia.

139
00:11:11,141 --> 00:11:16,060
The technology was very much World War I technology on the Shenandoah.

140
00:11:16,560 --> 00:11:19,240
There were some American refinements and improvements,

141
00:11:19,540 --> 00:11:21,400
and of course it flew with helium, not hydrogen.

142
00:11:22,160 --> 00:11:26,680
Helium is an inert gas first discovered in a few Texas oil wells.

143
00:11:27,240 --> 00:11:31,220
In 1922, the gas was in short supply and very expensive.

144
00:11:32,340 --> 00:11:35,120
And even though helium provided less lift than hydrogen,

145
00:11:35,300 --> 00:11:37,840
its primary advantage was that it does not burn.

146
00:11:37,841 --> 00:11:41,500
The golden age of airships had begun.

147
00:11:42,260 --> 00:11:45,160
The Shenandoah's first flight from Lakehurst, New Jersey,

148
00:11:45,280 --> 00:11:48,580
marked the beginning of the era of American rigid airships.

149
00:11:49,420 --> 00:11:52,600
Americans were enchanted by the ship's flights around the country.

150
00:11:53,080 --> 00:11:56,620
She was hailed as the forerunner to a commercial passenger airship.

151
00:11:57,140 --> 00:11:59,120
However, her service was cut short.

152
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:05,460
In 1925, the Shenandoah got caught in a severe Midwest thunderstorm and broke in half.

153
00:12:05,461 --> 00:12:11,620
The 210-foot-long forward section carrying seven men shot up to 10,000 feet.

154
00:12:12,180 --> 00:12:18,500
Lieutenant Commander Charles Rosendahl figured he could fly what was left of the airship under the same principles as a balloon.

155
00:12:19,140 --> 00:12:27,800
By dumping gasoline and valving off helium, he was able to fly what was left of the ship for almost two hours before the forward section of the Shenandoah crashed.

156
00:12:28,620 --> 00:12:32,200
While badly injured, Rosendahl and his six men survived.

157
00:12:32,201 --> 00:12:34,480
Fourteen others died.

158
00:12:35,460 --> 00:12:49,460
In October of 1924, Americans were able to get their first look at their second rigid airship, the German-built LZ-126.

159
00:12:51,860 --> 00:13:00,341
This was the state-of-the-art airship design for the 1920s, and the Germans believed it would be the last up when they would ever build, so they put extra care into

160
00:13:00,342 --> 00:13:01,220
its construction.

161
00:13:01,221 --> 00:13:03,535
The LZ-126 was the vision of Dr.

162
00:13:03,536 --> 00:13:12,000
Hugo Eckner, a business savvy and cautious Zeppelin pilot who took over the company after Count von Zeppelin's death.

163
00:13:12,560 --> 00:13:19,100
His favorite task was using the Zeppelin as a means of increasing friendship between nations.

164
00:13:19,300 --> 00:13:25,920
To him, the Zeppelin provided this means of international trade and commerce.

165
00:13:25,921 --> 00:13:32,920
Echner was a very unique businessman, and that is very evident through the LZ-126,

166
00:13:32,921 --> 00:13:37,920
because when the ship was completed and due to make its flight to the United States,

167
00:13:37,921 --> 00:13:40,920
no insurance company was willing to take the risk.

168
00:13:40,921 --> 00:13:45,920
But he was so sure of the technology of that airship and that the flight would be successful

169
00:13:45,921 --> 00:13:48,920
that he said, OK, we will insure the flight ourselves.

170
00:13:48,921 --> 00:13:53,920
Upon his arrival in the States, Echner took a leisurely flight over Manhattan

171
00:13:53,921 --> 00:13:57,920
before delivering the ship to the Navy base in Lakehurst, New Jersey.

172
00:13:58,920 --> 00:14:03,660
The Navy immediately replaced the ship's hydrogen with non-flammable helium

173
00:14:03,661 --> 00:14:05,580
and renamed her Los Angeles.

174
00:14:07,560 --> 00:14:10,200
You know, I see this thing in front of the hangar coming in.

175
00:14:10,600 --> 00:14:12,800
I didn't know what the heck I saw. It was so big.

176
00:14:13,720 --> 00:14:16,860
I mean, and it looked like a mountain was floating in there.

177
00:14:17,640 --> 00:14:21,760
It was given to the Navy to use for experimental and training purposes.

178
00:14:21,761 --> 00:14:26,660
and it wasn't supposed to be used for war purposes or anything like that.

179
00:14:27,060 --> 00:14:31,940
The Los Angeles went on to have a long and distinguished career as a Navy training ship.

180
00:14:32,540 --> 00:14:37,500
She flew more than 4,000 hours, the most of any American rigid airship.

181
00:14:39,700 --> 00:14:44,180
While the Germans were trying to reestablish a tradition of building rigid airships,

182
00:14:44,181 --> 00:14:46,500
the British were having trouble with their designs.

183
00:14:46,501 --> 00:14:49,500
Using downed zeppelins from World War I as models,

184
00:14:49,501 --> 00:14:51,500
their technology was dated.

185
00:14:51,501 --> 00:14:56,500
Nevertheless, in 1930, the British rolled out one of the most unique airships to date.

186
00:14:56,501 --> 00:15:01,500
The R101 featured promenades and panoramic views.

187
00:15:01,501 --> 00:15:06,500
At the last minute, an additional airbag was needed in order to lift the lavish ship.

188
00:15:06,501 --> 00:15:13,500
The British seemed to have a flair for putting on too many things that were not necessary.

189
00:15:13,501 --> 00:15:18,500
A ship's bell, for example, that probably weighed, you know, 20, 40 pounds.

190
00:15:18,501 --> 00:15:21,500
Things like that added up very quickly.

191
00:15:21,501 --> 00:15:26,500
54 passengers boarded the R101 for her maiden voyage to India.

192
00:15:26,501 --> 00:15:32,500
150 miles from England, the airship crashed in the French countryside, killing 48 people,

193
00:15:32,501 --> 00:15:35,500
as well as the British rigid airship program.

194
00:15:38,500 --> 00:15:42,500
The American airship program, on the other hand, was about to change.

195
00:15:42,501 --> 00:15:46,100
Rich in helium, yet lacking the necessary technology,

196
00:15:46,101 --> 00:15:49,600
the Americans went into business with the airship experienced Germans

197
00:15:49,601 --> 00:15:52,600
forming the Goodyear Zeppelin Company.

198
00:15:52,601 --> 00:15:55,800
Here was an American firm who believed in building zeppelins.

199
00:15:55,801 --> 00:15:59,100
P.W. Litchfield, who was the president of Goodyear,

200
00:15:59,101 --> 00:16:01,400
was very much an airship advocate,

201
00:16:01,401 --> 00:16:05,100
and he negotiated to acquire the zeppelin patents.

202
00:16:05,101 --> 00:16:07,400
They not only acquired the patent rights,

203
00:16:07,401 --> 00:16:10,600
but they acquired 13 of their,

204
00:16:10,601 --> 00:16:13,640
of the Zeppelin Company's leading technologists.

205
00:16:13,641 --> 00:16:16,680
They began work immediately on the USS Akron,

206
00:16:16,681 --> 00:16:20,520
the most innovative airship of her time.

207
00:16:20,521 --> 00:16:23,720
Construction started in the autumn of 1929,

208
00:16:23,721 --> 00:16:27,120
and it was finished by the summer of 1931.

209
00:16:27,121 --> 00:16:29,560
She was a battleship of the sky.

210
00:16:29,561 --> 00:16:33,160
The USS Akron was the first ship, and that was different

211
00:16:33,161 --> 00:16:34,920
in that it was designed from the outset

212
00:16:34,921 --> 00:16:37,120
to be a flying aircraft carrier.

213
00:16:37,121 --> 00:16:39,800
Inside the belly of the airship, there was a hangar bay,

214
00:16:39,801 --> 00:16:42,140
and it could carry up to five biplanes.

215
00:16:42,141 --> 00:16:45,340
They had several planes, but the most famous was the Sparrowhawk,

216
00:16:45,341 --> 00:16:49,940
a little bit of a 20-foot wingspan fighter.

217
00:16:49,941 --> 00:16:51,980
It would come up, had a hook on the top,

218
00:16:51,981 --> 00:16:55,820
and it would hook onto a trapeze bar from a trapeze

219
00:16:55,821 --> 00:16:57,280
and lowered from the ship.

220
00:16:57,281 --> 00:17:00,780
It would engage, and then the trapeze would raise the ship up

221
00:17:00,781 --> 00:17:04,500
and stow it in a hangar.

222
00:17:04,501 --> 00:17:07,320
And the idea behind this was that the planes could fly out

223
00:17:07,321 --> 00:17:10,760
and scout at a much greater distance than the airship alone.

224
00:17:11,240 --> 00:17:15,040
The Ekron had three reinforced keels and eight engines

225
00:17:15,041 --> 00:17:18,620
capable of driving the ship at nearly 70 miles per hour.

226
00:17:19,600 --> 00:17:21,580
The engines were inside the ship,

227
00:17:22,240 --> 00:17:24,460
and a log shaft came out there on the propellers,

228
00:17:24,720 --> 00:17:29,080
and you just walk through a door into the engine compartment.

229
00:17:29,720 --> 00:17:34,600
In 1933, after logging more than 1,700 flight hours,

230
00:17:34,601 --> 00:17:41,000
the 200-ton airship flew into a storm off the New Jersey coast and hit the water at full speed.

231
00:17:41,800 --> 00:17:46,600
73 of the 76 men perished. There were no life jackets on board.

232
00:17:48,360 --> 00:17:52,760
Barely two weeks after the crash of the Akron, her sister ship was completed.

233
00:17:52,761 --> 00:17:58,360
The USS Macon was identical except that she was 8,000 pounds lighter and faster,

234
00:17:58,920 --> 00:18:01,960
a fact the new commanding officer found difficult to deal with.

235
00:18:01,961 --> 00:18:09,280
As one senior airship officer remarked to me, he was so careful it was downright embarrassing.

236
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:13,960
And you can't really blame him. I mean, he takes command of a ship.

237
00:18:14,200 --> 00:18:16,940
The preceding ship was destroyed only two weeks before.

238
00:18:17,900 --> 00:18:21,880
Built to cruise 10,000 miles without refueling, she's the pride of the Navy.

239
00:18:22,360 --> 00:18:29,900
Using its fighter planes as lookouts, the Macon could patrol almost 129,000 square miles of ocean in a day.

240
00:18:29,901 --> 00:18:38,480
In February of 1935, the Macon was lost in severe weather off the coast of California near Point Sur.

241
00:18:40,380 --> 00:18:46,280
This time, they had life jackets on board and all but two of the 83 men on board survived.

242
00:18:47,880 --> 00:18:55,960
When the Macon sank, the Navy was not interested enough in wanting to recover or do anything about bringing any of it back up.

243
00:18:55,961 --> 00:18:58,540
They figured they'd spent enough money down the drain.

244
00:18:59,460 --> 00:19:02,180
And had gotten nothing really from it, except wrecks.

245
00:19:04,840 --> 00:19:13,420
The USS Los Angeles was retired in 1932 after eight years of service and a total of 331 flights.

246
00:19:13,421 --> 00:19:27,421
On July 8th, 1928, the LZ-127, or Graf Zeppelin, was christened by Count von Zeppelin's daughter.

247
00:19:30,060 --> 00:19:34,640
Nearly 10 stories high, the ship cleared the hangar door by only two feet.

248
00:19:35,580 --> 00:19:38,340
With the easing of sanctions imposed by the Versailles Treaty,

249
00:19:38,460 --> 00:19:41,040
the Germans were back in the Zeppelin business for themselves.

250
00:19:41,041 --> 00:19:46,040
At the helm was Dr. Hugo Eckner, the Zeppelin's greatest champion.

251
00:19:46,041 --> 00:19:49,040
This was the period of empire building.

252
00:19:49,041 --> 00:19:55,040
The vision of Dr. Eckner was to see an intercontinental airship passenger service.

253
00:19:55,041 --> 00:20:02,040
At 775 feet, the Graf Zeppelin was two and a half football fields long.

254
00:20:02,041 --> 00:20:10,040
And in early October, she set out on the first trans-oceanic voyage by an airship with passengers.

255
00:20:11,040 --> 00:20:16,000
It was designed to prove the airworthiness of rigid airships.

256
00:20:16,001 --> 00:20:21,760
That was Dr. Eckner's primary concern because you have to remember at this time

257
00:20:21,761 --> 00:20:27,680
the early airlines they were starting to make a name for themselves and so airships

258
00:20:27,681 --> 00:20:33,560
due to the the dangerous nature of hydrogen due to their size tended to be

259
00:20:33,561 --> 00:20:39,400
still considered as a frail means of transportation and so the 127 was really

260
00:20:39,401 --> 00:20:41,800
a very experimental airship.

261
00:20:41,801 --> 00:20:44,260
Equipped with two sets of gas cells,

262
00:20:44,261 --> 00:20:47,800
one for engine fuel and another for lifting gas,

263
00:20:47,801 --> 00:20:51,580
the Graf Zeppelin arrived safely in the United States.

264
00:20:51,581 --> 00:20:53,700
The airship was met with great fanfare

265
00:20:53,701 --> 00:20:57,980
as thousands of people flocked to get a glimpse of her.

266
00:20:57,981 --> 00:21:00,720
In bold pursuit of his dream of regular commercial

267
00:21:00,721 --> 00:21:03,040
transcontinental airship service,

268
00:21:03,041 --> 00:21:07,100
Hugo Eckner vowed to fly the Graf Zeppelin around the world,

269
00:21:07,101 --> 00:21:09,720
Not in a fictional 80 days, but in two weeks,

270
00:21:10,420 --> 00:21:13,500
the trip was projected to cost more than $250,000,

271
00:21:14,120 --> 00:21:16,520
money the Zeppelin company didn't have.

272
00:21:17,640 --> 00:21:19,800
As a friend of his once remarked to me,

273
00:21:20,180 --> 00:21:23,000
you know it's remarkable that he kept that company going

274
00:21:23,001 --> 00:21:25,800
at the depths of the Depression, particularly in Germany.

275
00:21:26,100 --> 00:21:29,180
So he knew how to get money and make money and the like.

276
00:21:29,181 --> 00:21:32,860
So too did newspaper magnate William Randolph Hearst,

277
00:21:33,260 --> 00:21:36,460
who immediately offered $150,000 to Eckner

278
00:21:36,461 --> 00:21:38,520
for the exclusive rights to cover the trip.

279
00:21:39,340 --> 00:21:42,100
Incredibly, the rest of the money came from stamp collectors.

280
00:21:42,880 --> 00:21:45,140
Notices would appear even in the New York Times

281
00:21:45,141 --> 00:21:47,700
or the papers in this country and everywhere else

282
00:21:47,701 --> 00:21:50,140
that the ship was going to make this record flight,

283
00:21:50,300 --> 00:21:53,000
and now is your chance, everyone, to get some mail

284
00:21:53,001 --> 00:21:56,680
with a special stamp on it carried on the Graf Zeppelin.

285
00:22:00,360 --> 00:22:02,120
The flight of the Graf Zeppelin

286
00:22:02,121 --> 00:22:05,260
provided classic newsreel images seen worldwide.

287
00:22:06,460 --> 00:22:13,100
magic of motion pictures you will share with us the thrills and enjoyment of perhaps the greatest

288
00:22:13,101 --> 00:22:24,780
aerial venturer in all history we are now well out over the atlantic we are making 80 miles per hour

289
00:22:25,580 --> 00:22:34,140
this is the dining room comfortable even luxurious worthy of any first class hotel on land

290
00:22:36,460 --> 00:22:39,840
And now, as we sail, let's listen a while.

291
00:22:52,100 --> 00:22:53,640
England at last.

292
00:22:54,020 --> 00:22:57,200
The Graf crossed the Atlantic in a record 42 hours

293
00:22:57,201 --> 00:22:59,400
and continued on her way to Germany.

294
00:23:00,480 --> 00:23:04,300
Berlin, where thousands in the streets below

295
00:23:04,301 --> 00:23:07,300
rejoice over the Zeppelin's triumph.

296
00:23:10,620 --> 00:23:14,800
The land of the rising sun in sight.

297
00:23:26,900 --> 00:23:30,900
And now for the unexplored skies of the Pacific.

298
00:23:30,901 --> 00:23:35,500
on the first non-stop flight ever made.

299
00:23:36,640 --> 00:23:41,140
Most of the time we fly in a strange uncanny universe

300
00:23:41,141 --> 00:23:44,480
with limitless seas of close-packed clouds,

301
00:23:45,540 --> 00:23:48,300
below a world in which time means little

302
00:23:48,301 --> 00:23:52,680
and the ordinary perceptions of distance even less.

303
00:23:53,700 --> 00:23:56,420
Our first glimpse of American territory.

304
00:24:00,900 --> 00:24:07,400
Directly over the Golden Gate, the sleek silver airship approaches San Francisco.

305
00:24:08,960 --> 00:24:13,260
The graph flies very, very low over the heart of the city.

306
00:24:14,800 --> 00:24:20,480
Occasionally we are shaken up a bit, but we float safely over the mountains of the great southwest.

307
00:24:22,660 --> 00:24:27,020
What an inspiring view of the grandeur that is America we are getting.

308
00:24:27,021 --> 00:24:34,780
A voyage such as we have made is truly not only an education and geography, but a lesson in patriotism.

309
00:24:37,660 --> 00:24:42,660
Chicago is giving us as great a thrill as the Zeppelin is giving Chicago.

310
00:24:44,520 --> 00:24:49,820
In 12 flying days, we have traveled some 20,000 miles,

311
00:24:49,821 --> 00:24:57,720
spanned two continents, looked down upon ten nations, conquered mountains and sea,

312
00:24:58,160 --> 00:25:01,380
and here we are, journey's end in sight.

313
00:25:05,480 --> 00:25:08,940
After successfully completing a trip around the world,

314
00:25:09,100 --> 00:25:11,520
Dr. Eckner was hailed in New York as a hero

315
00:25:11,521 --> 00:25:14,240
and considered one of the most famous men in the world.

316
00:25:14,241 --> 00:25:23,240
Indeed it had.

317
00:25:23,241 --> 00:25:29,840
The 7,000-mile nonstop flight from Germany to Japan was proof positive for many airship

318
00:25:29,841 --> 00:25:31,840
skeptics.

319
00:25:31,841 --> 00:25:36,400
Soon Eckner pioneered passenger service to South America.

320
00:25:36,401 --> 00:25:40,140
Only the well-to-do could afford a flight on an airship.

321
00:25:40,141 --> 00:25:44,220
The early flights, or these flights to North and South America, cost approximately 1,000

322
00:25:44,221 --> 00:25:52,080
Reismark, which was several years' income for a normal German worker at the time.

323
00:25:52,081 --> 00:25:56,140
It only took two and a half days to North America and three and a half days to South

324
00:25:56,141 --> 00:25:58,960
America, nonstop.

325
00:25:58,961 --> 00:26:04,580
Even the airplanes from Pan Am were not able to make this type of a trip.

326
00:26:04,581 --> 00:26:08,000
It was the fastest means of getting there.

327
00:26:08,001 --> 00:26:13,700
With still more skies to fly, Eckner set off on a scientific expedition to the North Pole

328
00:26:13,701 --> 00:26:16,380
in July of 1931.

329
00:26:16,381 --> 00:26:20,900
Not only did they bring back information about the Arctic's atmosphere, but the Graf Zeppelin

330
00:26:20,901 --> 00:26:24,620
proved she could fly in extreme conditions.

331
00:26:24,621 --> 00:26:29,060
Here again, cold weather didn't affect the operation of the airship at all.

332
00:26:29,061 --> 00:26:35,340
And so that was the big success and the big reasoning behind the 127.

333
00:26:35,341 --> 00:26:41,680
that, Eckner could prove and safely say, we can start an intercontinental passenger airship service

334
00:26:41,681 --> 00:26:42,934
and make it work.

335
00:26:42,935 --> 00:26:48,980
While the Graf Zeppelin ruled the skies, Hitler and the Nazi party were navigating

336
00:26:48,981 --> 00:26:50,078
to rule Germany.

337
00:26:50,079 --> 00:26:55,840
If Eckner was to continue building his dream, he would soon have to make a deal with

338
00:26:55,841 --> 00:26:56,700
the devil.

339
00:26:56,701 --> 00:27:04,440
The Graf Zeppelin crossed the ocean 144 times and flew more than one million accident-free

340
00:27:04,441 --> 00:27:06,440
miles.

341
00:27:13,440 --> 00:27:17,440
By 1933, Hitler and the Nazi Party controlled Germany.

342
00:27:17,441 --> 00:27:21,440
They also controlled the fate of Dr. Eckner and his dream

343
00:27:21,441 --> 00:27:24,440
of intercontinental airship service.

344
00:27:24,441 --> 00:27:29,440
I hope I will be able in about six or seven weeks

345
00:27:29,441 --> 00:27:35,660
weeks to take over the new airship to the United States of America.

346
00:27:36,540 --> 00:27:42,740
He was asked at one time to let Hitler speak at a political rally in the German Zeppelin

347
00:27:42,741 --> 00:27:45,800
company hangars at Friedrichshafen, and he refused.

348
00:27:46,760 --> 00:27:50,600
Well, you didn't do that and still remain very friendly with the Nazis.

349
00:27:51,980 --> 00:27:56,880
Eckner was an outspoken critic of the Nazi party and was only spared from the concentration

350
00:27:56,881 --> 00:28:00,720
camps because of his popularity throughout the world.

351
00:28:00,721 --> 00:28:03,520
Dr. Eckner did not like the Nazis.

352
00:28:03,521 --> 00:28:05,280
That is very obvious.

353
00:28:05,281 --> 00:28:09,520
But on the other hand, he saw the necessity to play with the devil, if you will.

354
00:28:09,521 --> 00:28:10,708
And that gave Dr.

355
00:28:10,709 --> 00:28:16,160
Eckner the funds that he needed to begin his intercontinental airship service.

356
00:28:17,600 --> 00:28:19,360
The deal was this.

357
00:28:19,361 --> 00:28:23,840
Eckner needed money to build bigger hangars that could house an entire fleet of airships

358
00:28:23,841 --> 00:28:26,340
large enough to service the major cities of the world.

359
00:28:27,200 --> 00:28:29,380
Hitler agreed to finance Eckner's plans

360
00:28:29,381 --> 00:28:31,000
and formed an operating company

361
00:28:31,001 --> 00:28:33,220
which would control the airships out of Frankfurt.

362
00:28:35,040 --> 00:28:38,180
Early in 1936, one of Eckner's dream ships

363
00:28:38,181 --> 00:28:41,020
became a reality with the unveiling of the Hindenburg,

364
00:28:41,460 --> 00:28:44,240
the epitome of everything the Germans had learned

365
00:28:44,241 --> 00:28:45,680
about rigid airships.

366
00:28:45,681 --> 00:28:48,740
She was the largest airship the world would ever see.

367
00:28:49,900 --> 00:28:52,460
At a length of 804 feet,

368
00:28:52,461 --> 00:29:00,520
She was 572 feet longer than a 747 jumbo jet and only 78 feet shorter than the Titanic.

369
00:29:01,240 --> 00:29:05,940
She weighed 130 tons and was as high as a 13-story building.

370
00:29:07,400 --> 00:29:15,160
The Hindenburg had a gas capacity of more than 7 million cubic feet, the volume of 300 average American homes.

371
00:29:16,060 --> 00:29:19,400
She was powered by four Daimler-Benz diesel engines

372
00:29:19,401 --> 00:29:22,460
capable of 1,320 horsepower each.

373
00:29:23,920 --> 00:29:28,600
Her aluminum alloy girders laid end-to-end would stretch for 13 miles.

374
00:29:29,760 --> 00:29:35,740
To Eckner's dismay, however, huge swastikas emblazoned the ship's 105-foot tail fins.

375
00:29:36,420 --> 00:29:40,520
And it wasn't long before the Hindenburg and the beloved Graf Zeppelin

376
00:29:40,521 --> 00:29:42,840
went to work for the Nazi party.

377
00:29:42,841 --> 00:29:48,840
The Nazis needed the airships for propaganda purposes with the big swastikas on the tail,

378
00:29:48,841 --> 00:29:51,840
and Eckner had a fit about that.

379
00:29:51,841 --> 00:29:57,840
Shortly after the Hindenburg was completed, the airships traveled to German cities of 100,000 or more,

380
00:29:57,841 --> 00:30:01,840
dropping leaflets promoting Hitler and Nazi Party platforms.

381
00:30:01,841 --> 00:30:05,840
They continued to exploit the massive airship for political purposes

382
00:30:05,841 --> 00:30:11,840
at the Nazi Party's Congress in Nuremberg and the 1936 Olympic Games in Berlin.

383
00:30:12,840 --> 00:30:17,680
The Nazis replaced Eckner as the ship's cautious and conservative captain

384
00:30:17,681 --> 00:30:20,620
in favor of a pilot that was more politically acceptable,

385
00:30:21,140 --> 00:30:23,400
a move that didn't sit well with Eckner

386
00:30:23,401 --> 00:30:26,440
when the new pilot damaged the Hindenburg's tail fin.

387
00:30:27,960 --> 00:30:30,080
Eckner storms up to Lehmann,

388
00:30:30,260 --> 00:30:33,760
who was the captain in command of it at that time,

389
00:30:34,120 --> 00:30:35,820
and in a loud voice said,

390
00:30:35,920 --> 00:30:38,320
how could you take our brand new airship out

391
00:30:38,321 --> 00:30:42,440
just to please your lord and master Adolf Hitler?

392
00:30:42,840 --> 00:30:47,080
As a result, the press people were informed that from henceforth,

393
00:30:47,580 --> 00:30:52,100
Eckner's name and picture were not ever to be mentioned in the Nazi press again.

394
00:30:52,920 --> 00:30:57,020
So he was quite a brave fellow in that respect.

395
00:31:00,260 --> 00:31:05,080
When Hindenburg made its first trip to this country in 1936,

396
00:31:05,660 --> 00:31:08,620
Eckner was on board, but he had no title and no authority.

397
00:31:08,780 --> 00:31:10,440
They went around him, essentially.

398
00:31:10,441 --> 00:31:14,540
Of all the man-made birds that fly, this was the greatest.

399
00:31:15,600 --> 00:31:19,460
The Hindenburg was built above all for intercontinental flight,

400
00:31:19,780 --> 00:31:21,420
and eventually that's what she did.

401
00:31:22,060 --> 00:31:26,540
In 1936, the airship made seven scheduled round trips to South America

402
00:31:26,541 --> 00:31:28,340
and ten to North America.

403
00:31:29,600 --> 00:31:33,180
The Hindenburg was the largest man-made thing that ever flew,

404
00:31:33,720 --> 00:31:38,360
and it normally flew at altitudes of 1,000 feet or less.

405
00:31:38,361 --> 00:31:47,280
Now, when you had something three-city blockless long flying directly overhead, making very little noise, it left a tremendous impression.

406
00:31:48,140 --> 00:31:55,200
Just as Eckner had imagined, the Hindenburgs passenger accommodations set a new standard in aircraft luxury.

407
00:31:56,480 --> 00:31:59,140
They had every comfort dreamable on board.

408
00:32:00,040 --> 00:32:03,100
Gourmet meals were cooked in an electric kitchen.

409
00:32:03,101 --> 00:32:08,100
The passengers had two bunks, or two beds in a room.

410
00:32:08,101 --> 00:32:11,100
Each room was equipped with hot and cold running water.

411
00:32:11,101 --> 00:32:12,100
There was also a shower.

412
00:32:12,101 --> 00:32:15,100
There was a writing room, which also had a piano on board.

413
00:32:15,101 --> 00:32:17,100
Of course, the piano was out of aluminum

414
00:32:17,101 --> 00:32:20,100
and didn't weigh more than about 40 pounds.

415
00:32:20,101 --> 00:32:23,100
The Hindenburg even had its own special smoking room,

416
00:32:23,101 --> 00:32:27,100
which was pressurized to prevent any hydrogen gas

417
00:32:27,101 --> 00:32:29,100
from entering the room.

418
00:32:29,101 --> 00:32:32,100
In the smoking room was one lighter,

419
00:32:32,101 --> 00:32:34,420
which was shared among all smokers,

420
00:32:34,520 --> 00:32:36,580
and you could only smoke with inside that room,

421
00:32:36,800 --> 00:32:39,200
which made it, of course, a very unique airship.

422
00:32:40,100 --> 00:32:43,480
Passengers paid $400 one way to North America

423
00:32:43,481 --> 00:32:45,780
and $720 round trip.

424
00:32:47,300 --> 00:32:49,420
Filled with highly flammable hydrogen,

425
00:32:49,600 --> 00:32:51,160
the crew wore felt booties

426
00:32:51,161 --> 00:32:53,940
to prevent even the smallest spark from friction.

427
00:32:55,020 --> 00:32:57,340
But attention to the smallest detail

428
00:32:57,341 --> 00:33:00,080
would not be enough to prevent Hugo Eckner's dream

429
00:33:00,081 --> 00:33:01,660
from going up in smoke.

430
00:33:02,100 --> 00:33:15,400
Only a handful of reporters were present in 1937 when the Hindenburg made her final approach to the Navy base in Lakehurst, New Jersey.

431
00:33:15,780 --> 00:33:21,140
After all, Eckner's dream ship had made 17 transcontinental flights the year before.

432
00:33:21,840 --> 00:33:25,380
John Ionicone was working that day on the Navy ground crew.

433
00:33:25,900 --> 00:33:32,040
He was one of 198 sailors on station and the only military man still alive who saw what happened.

434
00:33:32,100 --> 00:33:36,700
They were supposed to come in at 6 o'clock that morning, but it was 12 hours late.

435
00:33:37,040 --> 00:33:42,940
It had been raining and thunder and lightning and a lot of clouds crammed off to the base.

436
00:33:43,480 --> 00:33:44,500
We wouldn't let them land.

437
00:33:44,800 --> 00:33:48,660
And so they just flew around, and then around 6 o'clock they told them to come back

438
00:33:48,661 --> 00:33:50,620
because it had started to clear up.

439
00:33:51,080 --> 00:33:52,520
In the meantime, the ship got all wet.

440
00:33:52,840 --> 00:33:58,460
They were coming in for a landing, and we could see the whole ship from where we were all the time.

441
00:33:58,461 --> 00:34:03,460
She dropped the lines and the ground crew had a hold of the two nose lines.

442
00:34:03,461 --> 00:34:06,460
It was a routine land. Everything was routine.

443
00:34:06,461 --> 00:34:11,460
And then all of a sudden right there in front of the tail I saw a big red glow inside the ship.

444
00:34:11,461 --> 00:34:15,460
And then it broke through the cover and it just burned.

445
00:34:15,461 --> 00:34:17,460
And it's crashing. It's crashing terrible.

446
00:34:17,461 --> 00:34:19,460
Oh my, get out of the way, please.

447
00:34:19,461 --> 00:34:23,460
It's burning, bursting into flames and it's falling on the morning fast.

448
00:34:23,461 --> 00:34:25,460
And all the folks between us, this is terrible.

449
00:34:25,461 --> 00:34:27,460
This is one of the worst catastrophes in the world.

450
00:34:27,461 --> 00:34:38,321
Oh, it's a place running, oh, four or five hundred feet into the sky, and it's a terrific crash, ladies and gentlemen, the smoke is enslaved now, and the framers

451
00:34:38,322 --> 00:34:44,460
crashing to the ground, not quite to the mooring mass, and all the humanity, and all the...

452
00:34:45,460 --> 00:34:52,460
I saw one man jump out of the nose, and when it was up there about 400 feet, and he got killed.

453
00:34:52,461 --> 00:34:53,178
killed.

454
00:34:53,179 --> 00:35:01,380
We saw one man walk out and he had, he was burnt from the top of his head to his

455
00:35:01,381 --> 00:35:06,380
heels. All he had on was his shoes.

456
00:35:06,381 --> 00:35:13,380
When we got, we got to the passenger compartment,

457
00:35:13,381 --> 00:35:14,909
I mean, there was people still in it.

458
00:35:14,910 --> 00:35:17,100
We told them to get out and we started looking around

459
00:35:17,101 --> 00:35:18,240
for some more.

460
00:35:18,241 --> 00:35:19,500
They wasn't even scratched.

461
00:35:21,840 --> 00:35:25,280
The Hindenburg, which took four years to build,

462
00:35:25,281 --> 00:35:28,280
was destroyed in approximately 37 seconds.

463
00:35:31,440 --> 00:35:34,660
Film of the Hindenburg crash was not shown in Germany

464
00:35:34,661 --> 00:35:36,560
until after World War II.

465
00:35:36,561 --> 00:35:50,561
What was but a moment ago, the queen of the air is now a shell of consuming flame, a tragic memory.

466
00:35:54,680 --> 00:36:01,840
On the night of May 6, 1937, the greatest airship ever built lay in a smoldering, twisted heap.

467
00:36:01,841 --> 00:36:06,540
I stood watch on it that night, tried to keep the looters out of it.

468
00:36:06,640 --> 00:36:08,260
But you couldn't do it. I mean, everybody's coming in,

469
00:36:08,280 --> 00:36:10,020
they want a little piece of this, a little piece of that.

470
00:36:11,800 --> 00:36:14,000
Miraculously, 62 people survived.

471
00:36:14,340 --> 00:36:19,740
The Americans and Germans held hearings to investigate the cause of the crash that killed 35.

472
00:36:21,060 --> 00:36:23,620
The Germans said it was sabotaged, most of them,

473
00:36:23,700 --> 00:36:28,600
because they could not bring themselves to a frame of mind to admit

474
00:36:28,601 --> 00:36:31,840
that there could be any deficiency in the airship.

475
00:36:32,700 --> 00:36:34,020
Eckner was not the same way.

476
00:36:34,260 --> 00:36:37,040
I talked to him at some length about what did he,

477
00:36:37,160 --> 00:36:38,860
the greatest airship man of all time,

478
00:36:38,960 --> 00:36:41,000
think about what destroyed his airship.

479
00:36:41,640 --> 00:36:43,900
And he was convinced it made a sharp turn

480
00:36:43,901 --> 00:36:45,740
before it, as it approached the mast,

481
00:36:46,300 --> 00:36:48,360
he thinks that there was a broken wire

482
00:36:48,361 --> 00:36:50,240
that slashed open a gas cell.

483
00:36:51,080 --> 00:36:55,760
That hydrogen escaped, caught and collected up inside the ship

484
00:36:55,761 --> 00:36:57,040
and started to leak out of it.

485
00:36:57,041 --> 00:37:04,120
and that the static electricity in the air was enough to set the thing off.

486
00:37:04,121 --> 00:37:10,000
I believe the Hindenburg crash as such marked the end of the airship

487
00:37:10,001 --> 00:37:14,740
as an effective passenger vehicle for intercontinental travel.

488
00:37:15,480 --> 00:37:18,640
You have to remember, airplanes had reached that magic point

489
00:37:18,641 --> 00:37:24,040
of technologically surpassing or at least achieving the same thing,

490
00:37:24,041 --> 00:37:27,400
even in a shorter period of time before, than the airships.

491
00:37:27,401 --> 00:37:30,680
So the days of the airship, the ridges, were numbered.

492
00:37:38,680 --> 00:37:44,680
With the start of World War II, the Nazis deflated and dismantled the remaining rigid airships.

493
00:37:44,681 --> 00:37:50,280
The once peaceful giants of the sky were scrapped and melted down for fighter planes.

494
00:37:50,281 --> 00:37:53,860
The U.S. Navy still was interested in airships,

495
00:37:53,861 --> 00:37:58,600
but knew that politically they could never build a really large one again.

496
00:37:58,601 --> 00:38:04,600
So they went instead to more blimps, small, non-rigid airships.

497
00:38:05,420 --> 00:38:08,240
A fraction the size of the rigid airships.

498
00:38:09,060 --> 00:38:11,680
Their job was patrolling for U-boats,

499
00:38:12,160 --> 00:38:16,380
and their squadrons were based all the way from Canada,

500
00:38:16,381 --> 00:38:21,500
down along the east coast, the Gulf Coast, in the Caribbean, down to Brazil,

501
00:38:21,680 --> 00:38:24,720
and we flew them to Africa, to the Mediterranean.

502
00:38:24,940 --> 00:38:27,700
They escorted something like 70,000 ships,

503
00:38:28,560 --> 00:38:32,700
with only two lost to an enemy submarine attack.

504
00:38:33,600 --> 00:38:38,100
After the war, Hugo Eckner served as a consultant to the Goodyear Company.

505
00:38:38,560 --> 00:38:41,120
Prior to his death in 1954, he said,

506
00:38:41,121 --> 00:38:43,880
The Zeppelin has given way to the airplane.

507
00:38:43,881 --> 00:38:46,600
a good thing has been replaced by a better.

508
00:38:52,460 --> 00:38:55,780
Today, blimps are used primarily for advertising

509
00:38:55,781 --> 00:38:58,860
and can still draw the attention of everybody in sight.

510
00:38:59,440 --> 00:39:03,300
While their sophisticated mechanics make them much easier to steer

511
00:39:03,301 --> 00:39:04,820
than the zeppelins of the past,

512
00:39:05,440 --> 00:39:08,600
blimps are nowhere near the size of the giant airships.

513
00:39:08,601 --> 00:39:15,900
An average blimp has about 100,000 cubic feet of gas, helium nowadays.

514
00:39:17,840 --> 00:39:20,800
The Hindenburg had 7.5 million cubic feet.

515
00:39:22,040 --> 00:39:25,820
Basically, a blimp today is about the length of the tail of the Hindenburg.

516
00:39:26,600 --> 00:39:31,820
A variety of blimps and tethered balloons are also used for communications platforms.

517
00:39:33,280 --> 00:39:37,160
Observation, radar, drug interdiction, and border patrols.

518
00:39:37,161 --> 00:39:43,060
They're all filled with non-flammable helium and seem to lack the romance, promise and

519
00:39:43,061 --> 00:39:46,260
adventure of the great rigid airships.

520
00:39:46,261 --> 00:39:52,540
More than 60 years after the crash of the Hindenburg, the Zeppelin company is building

521
00:39:52,541 --> 00:39:54,480
a revolutionary new airship.

522
00:39:54,481 --> 00:39:56,980
It's called the NT07.

523
00:39:56,981 --> 00:40:04,000
It is a rigid airship in design, although it has very little structure inside.

524
00:40:04,001 --> 00:40:09,320
The new Zeppelin will seat 12 and have a top speed of 90 miles an hour.

525
00:40:09,321 --> 00:40:14,900
Due to the airship's mechanical agility, its primary use will be for sightseeing.

526
00:40:14,901 --> 00:40:20,760
The advantage of the new Zeppelin is incorporated in its tail thruster unit, which will allow

527
00:40:20,761 --> 00:40:31,320
the pilot to control the airship left, right, nose up and nose down at zero airspeed.

528
00:40:31,321 --> 00:40:36,120
This ability will reduce the number of ground crew needed on takeoff and landing.

529
00:40:36,121 --> 00:40:42,360
The ship's technical manager is a distant relative of Count von Zeppelin himself.

530
00:40:42,361 --> 00:40:47,340
Five Zeppelins have already been sold for $6.8 million each.

531
00:40:47,341 --> 00:40:53,420
The Germans will use the NT-07 for tourism, advertising and scientific research.

532
00:40:53,421 --> 00:40:57,660
The Swiss plan to use the new airship for sightseeing.

533
00:40:57,661 --> 00:41:04,160
Another German company plans to build an enormous rigid airship to transport heavy cargo to faraway places.

534
00:41:04,660 --> 00:41:13,420
As far as I see from their plans, if they get away with this, their airship would be even much, much larger than the Hindenburg ever was.

535
00:41:13,760 --> 00:41:19,400
In fact, the planned cargo lifter is three and a half times the length of a 747

536
00:41:19,401 --> 00:41:27,280
and will have a payload of more than four times that of a jumbo jet or 175 tons.

537
00:41:27,660 --> 00:41:32,660
Estimated cost, $60 million.

538
00:41:32,661 --> 00:41:37,660
Located on a former Soviet military base, work has already begun on the hangar,

539
00:41:37,661 --> 00:41:40,660
which will house two cargo lifters.

540
00:41:40,661 --> 00:41:47,660
The huge airships will utilize solar energy and use one-tenth the fuel of a traditional 747.

541
00:41:47,661 --> 00:41:53,660
It seems within the foreseeable future, at least, that we may indeed see the rebirth of the rigid airship.

542
00:41:53,661 --> 00:41:57,660
And that prospect holds the same promise as the original Zeppelin's.

543
00:41:57,661 --> 00:42:01,660
If you are lucky enough to ever see one, you will never forget it.

